LogTotal HOWTO

November 11, 2026
3 min read

“Snowflake logs” means one of two things, and the export works the same for both. It can be security data your team stores in Snowflake tables. Or it can be Snowflake’s own audit trail, the SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE views such as LOGIN_HISTORY and QUERY_HISTORY. For small pulls, run the query in SnowSQL with JSON output. For anything bigger, unload with COPY INTO a stage using FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = JSON), and download the files with GET.

Snowflake’s own logs drew a lot of attention in 2024, when attackers used stolen credentials to log in to customer accounts that didn’t enforce MFA. Login and query history are now standard asks in an investigation. Prime Core has more than 24,000 unique detection rules, mapped to 676 MITRE ATT&CK techniques. Below is how to get the data out, sanitize it, and investigate it for free in LogTotal.

Before you start

Access to ACCOUNT_USAGE. By default, only ACCOUNTADMIN can read these views. Other roles need the IMPORTED PRIVILEGES grant on the SNOWFLAKE database, or one of its database roles.

Latency and retention. ACCOUNT_USAGE views keep a year of history but lag behind by up to a couple of hours. For the last seven days with no lag, the INFORMATION_SCHEMA.LOGIN_HISTORY table function is an alternative.

A warehouse. Queries and unloads need a running warehouse, and they consume credits. Filter by time.

Option 1: SnowSQL with JSON output (small pulls)

snowsql -q "SELECT * FROM SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.LOGIN_HISTORY            WHERE EVENT_TIMESTAMP >= '2026-10-19' AND EVENT_TIMESTAMP < '2026-10-20'" \  -o output_format=json -o friendly=false -o timing=false > sf_logins_1019.jsonjq -c '.[]' sf_logins_1019.json > sf_logins_1019.jsonl

COPY INTO @~/exports/login_history_1019/FROM (  SELECT OBJECT_CONSTRUCT(*)  FROM SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.LOGIN_HISTORY  WHERE EVENT_TIMESTAMP >= '2026-10-19' AND EVENT_TIMESTAMP < '2026-10-20')FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = JSON COMPRESSION = NONE)OVERWRITE = TRUE;

JSON unloads need a single column, which is why the query wraps each row in OBJECT_CONSTRUCT(*). That function leaves out NULL fields. Then download from your user stage (@~):

GET @~/exports/login_history_1019/ file:///tmp/sf_export/;

and combine the files with cat /tmp/sf_export/* > sf_logins_1019.jsonl. The same pattern works for your own security tables. Swap in the table name and its timestamp column.

What to look for in login history

Look for failed logins (IS_SUCCESS = ‘NO’) in bursts, and password logins without a second factor (SECOND_AUTHENTICATION_FACTOR empty). Note logins from client IPs and client types you don’t recognize. Then follow those sessions into QUERY_HISTORY to see what was read.

Upload. Open logtotal.com and drop in the .jsonl file.

Sanitize.

Review.

When it goes wrong

SymptomLikely causeFixObject does not exist or not authorized on ACCOUNT_USAGERole lacks access to the SNOWFLAKE databaseAsk for IMPORTED PRIVILEGES or a database roleToday’s logins missingACCOUNT_USAGE latencyUse INFORMATION_SCHEMA.LOGIN_HISTORY for recent eventsCOPY INTO error about columnsJSON unload needs one columnWrap rows in OBJECT_CONSTRUCT(*)Fields missing in the JSONOBJECT_CONSTRUCT drops NULL valuesExpected; absent means NULLGET failsNot run from SnowSQL or the Snowflake CLIGET runs from a client, not the web UI

FAQ Can I do this in the web interface?Querying, yes. Downloads from the web interface aren’t JSON. The client route above gives you JSON lines.What about Snowflake event tables?Event tables hold logs and traces from your own functions and apps. Export them the same way as any table.

